2026-05-22T09:00Z claude created — gates v2 protocol round-2 review

--- body ---

## Context

Round-1 protocol review (PR #405 follow-up) had all four subject
agents flag same-vendor classifier bias as a top-line concern. The
v2 protocol spec (`experiments/sota/exp2_protocol_spec_v2.md`,
§4.1) substitutes `mistral-small-latest` with
**`nvidia/nemotron-nano-9b-v2`** — open-weight, NVIDIA, not
affiliated with any of the four subject vendors (Anthropic /
OpenAI / Mistral / Alibaba). Available via OpenRouter at
~$0.04/Mtok in, $0.16/Mtok out — cheaper than the Mistral-small
incumbent.

The substitution is **untested on this task**. v1 was at least
observed working on Mistral subject output; Nemotron is a new
component. Locking it in without calibration risks silently
moving where the harness fails.

## Calibration fixture (4 inputs)

Build a small fixture from existing smoke artefacts under
`experiments/outputs/sota_exp2_smoke/`. Each input is one assistant
response that has a known expected class:

| # | Input | Expected class | Source artefact |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Mistral planning preamble ("Understood! I will proceed in autonomous agentic mode...", 2474 chars, no table) | `no_report` | smoke v1 mistral_turn_02.report.md (the v1-round-2 planning preamble) |
| 2 | Mistral polished inventory (24568 chars, table + bibliography + sources populated) | `report` | smoke v1 mistral_turn_03.report.md |
| 3 | Mistral initial inventory draft (turn 2 v1, ~14688 chars, table fragment + bibliography) | `report` | smoke v1 mistral_turn_02.report.md (whichever is the initial inventory turn) |
| 4 | Synthesised verbose-planner response — multi-paragraph plan, lists intended sources, no table, no inventory rows | `no_report` | hand-crafted, ~1000 chars |

Pass criterion: Nemotron returns the expected class on all four.

## Procedure

1. Write `experiments/sota/calibrate_classifier.py` — small script:
   - Loads the four fixture inputs.
   - Dispatches each through the SAME classifier prompt template
     used in `experiments/sota/dialogue_classifier.py`.
   - Replaces the model id with `nvidia/nemotron-nano-9b-v2` via
     OpenRouter.
   - Records the verdict, wall-time, cost.
   - Compares against the expected class column.
2. Run it. Expected total cost: ~$0.01.
3. Three outcomes:
   - **All four pass** → lock Nemotron in
     `dialogue_classifier.py`'s `CLASSIFIER_MODEL` constant; v2
     round-2 review can proceed.
   - **3/4 pass, one borderline** → document the borderline case
     in the v2 spec (§4.1 already has a hedge clause); proceed.
   - **2/4 or fewer pass** → Nemotron is unsuitable; pick a
     fallback (Cohere Command, OpenAI gpt-5-nano, Llama-3.3
     instruct on OpenRouter, etc.); re-calibrate.

## Out of scope

- A full-distribution test (varying styles, languages, token counts).
  This is a smoke-level fixture, not a benchmark.
- Cost optimisation of the classifier surface — the classifier
  is harness overhead, not the experiment subject.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `experiments/sota/calibrate_classifier.py` exists.
- [ ] Run completes; result recorded in
      `experiments/outputs/classifier_calibration/nemotron_calibration.json`.
- [ ] Classifier model id locked in `dialogue_classifier.py`
      (either Nemotron after passing, or a fallback after
      documented failure).
- [ ] v2 spec §4.1 references the calibration result by commit
      SHA or filename.

## Blocks

- Round-2 protocol review (spec_v2.md dispatch to the four agents)
  is gated on this ticket closing.
